Notes about Debian's slapd package
----------------------------------

Starting with OpenLDAP 2.1, backend modules are compiled as dynamically
loadable shared objects. As a consequence thereof, you need to specify
the path of the modules and the modules to load in your slapd.conf file
for now. This can be done by lines such as the following:

modulepath      /usr/lib/ldap
moduleload      back_bdb

for the BDB database backend module.

The new BDB database backend is the recommended backend to use. In
comparison to the formerly recommended LDBM backend (which still exists
for compatibility reasons), it is supposed to be both faster and safer
to use, i.e. hot backups are now possible with the BDB database backend
and the like.

To switch to the BDB backend, you should export your current database
in LDIF format with the slapcat command (be sure to stop slapd before
doing so), move the old database files away, change /etc/ldap/slapd.conf,
and then reimport your database from the LDIF file via slapadd.

 -- Roland Bauerschmidt <rb@debian.org>  Wed, 19 Mar 2003 15:50:32 +0100

In the event that you are running slapd with a different back-end module
that depends on other programs (Such as an SQL database) you may need to
adjust the runlevels of slapd to start after the SQL database.

If you have need to create passwd/shadow/etc files from an LDAP
directory there is now a script included with these Debian packages
which may help you.  The script is in /usr/share/slapd/ and is named
'ldiftopasswd'.  In general you should be able to do:
ldapsearch | ldiftopasswd
and it will generate the files for you.  You will need appropriate
privleges, of course.

 -- Stephen Frost <sfrost@debian.org> Thu, 12 Jun 2003 12:42:52 -0400
